I dropped the four hypotheses whose condition is the edited parameter's own band: alpha|alpha (×2) and learning_rate_init|learning_rate_init (×2). None of the remaining ones disables the edited component.

From the rest, I kept one hypothesis per edit/condition pair. Where two shared a pair, I kept the one whose direction is more open to debate. For example, I kept "width up helps more at low LR" over "width up hurts at high LR".

{"hypotheses": [{"edit_param": "alpha", "edit_dir": "up", "cond_param": "batch_size", "cond_band": "low", "direction": "lower", "why": "sklearn scales the L2 term by alpha/batch_size per step, so at tiny batches raising alpha inflates an already large effective penalty and drives underfitting far more than at large batches."}, {"edit_param": "alpha", "edit_dir": "up", "cond_param": "depth", "cond_band": "high", "direction": "lower", "why": "Shrinkage of every layer compounds multiplicatively through three layers and attenuates the forward signal, so extra alpha damages deep nets more."}, {"edit_param": "width", "edit_dir": "up", "cond_param": "learning_rate_init", "cond_band": "low", "direction": "higher", "why": "Because Adam's per-weight step is width-independent, wider nets change their function faster at tiny LRs and so get further toward a good fit within the fixed 243 epochs."}, {"edit_param": "depth", "edit_dir": "up", "cond_param": "width", "cond_band": "low", "direction": "lower", "why": "Narrow layers act as information bottlenecks and lose a larger fraction of units to dead ReLUs, so stacking another narrow layer hurts more than adding a wide one."}, {"edit_param": "depth", "edit_dir": "up", "cond_param": "learning_rate_init", "cond_band": "high", "direction": "lower", "why": "Update-induced perturbations compound across layers, so an extra layer at already-aggressive LRs makes Adam training more likely to destabilize."}, {"edit_param": "learning_rate_init", "edit_dir": "up", "cond_param": "batch_size", "cond_band": "low", "direction": "lower", "why": "Tiny batches already take many noisy steps per epoch, so a larger step amplifies noise and overshoots, while large-batch runs are step-limited and benefit from a bigger LR."}, {"edit_param": "learning_rate_init", "edit_dir": "up", "cond_param": "width", "cond_band": "high", "direction": "lower", "why": "Wide layers sum many coordinated Adam updates so the per-step function change grows with width, making a higher LR overshoot sooner."}, {"edit_param": "batch_size", "edit_dir": "up", "cond_param": "learning_rate_init", "cond_band": "low", "direction": "lower", "why": "At tiny LRs the number of optimizer steps is the bottleneck, so cutting steps with a larger batch causes underfitting that does not occur when LR is adequate."}, {"edit_param": "batch_size", "edit_dir": "up", "cond_param": "alpha", "cond_band": "high", "direction": "higher", "why": "Because sklearn's effective L2 strength is alpha/batch_size, enlarging the batch directly relieves over-regularization when alpha is large."}, {"edit_param": "width", "edit_dir": "down", "cond_param": "depth", "cond_band": "high", "direction": "lower", "why": "Narrowing a three-layer net tightens several stacked bottlenecks at once, so lost capacity and dead-unit risk compound compared with a single hidden layer."}]}
